land, vfs: a landed mode or bits change is synced through its file's own handle in its batch's barrier before it is reported (A-472) -- fsync(2) flushes the metadata of the file it names and fdatasync need not; no source says a directory's sync covers another inode's mode

A metadata entry is staged in A-130's batch (stage, barrier, place): its fields set in place through its own handle,
the handle held open (one page against the batch's room), the barrier syncing the file whole (Linux fsync, macOS fsync,
Windows FlushFileBuffers through a handle that may write), and the entry reported and advanced after. A batch that
ends unsynced records its metadata entries as it records its files, never advancing them. A file the landing may not
open to set its metadata fails its entry alone.

